{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "/root/gated_mfrm_judge_bias/paper/introduction.tex:6:Existing methods address these distortions in isolation. Judge bias corrections---CyclicJudge \citep{cyclicjudge2026}, UDA \citep{uda2026}---remove systematic inter-judge differences without modeling contamination. Contamination detection tools---ConStat \citep{constat2024}, DCR \citep{dcr2025}, LogProber \citep{logprober2024}---adjust for memorization without accounting for judge effects (see Section~\ref{sec:related_work} for detailed review). A bias-first pipeline absorbs contamination-induced score inflation into ability estimates, because the bias model attributes all residual systematic variation to genuine ability. A contamination-first pipeline misattributes judge severity differences as contamination, because score elevation from a lenient judge is indistinguishable from elevation due to memorization without a joint model. In either ordering, the first correction stage produces estimates distorted by the unmodeled facet, and the second stage inherits this distortion.
